WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.850
Critics and viewers agree — Paul Dano's
masked Riddler in "The Batman" is terrifying.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.880
So why are fans cracking up over one of his
scenes?

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.629
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:10.629 --> 00:00:12.799
Heads up — spoilers ahead!

00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:16.650
Matt Reeves' "The Batman" offers up one of
the most realistic and frightening takes on

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.449
a DC Comics villain to date, with the Riddler
giving off vibes that are deeply reminiscent

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:22.539
of the Zodiac Killer.

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.220
"You know it's like, okay that's like a much
more sort of real…"

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.220
"Right.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.759
That's not question marks on a green suit."

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:31.759
"Yes."

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:34.570
But not everything about the "brilliant and
terrifying" character — as The Hollywood

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.910
Reporter describes him — is scary on the
surface.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:42.320
In fact, there's one unintentionally funny
scene involving the Riddler that comes toward

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.220
the end of "The Batman" that has many fans
laughing the more they think about it.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.270
The surprisingly comical Riddler scene is
found in the third and final act of the Warner

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.370
Brothers flick.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:56.140
Dano's Riddler is seen attempting to rally
online supporters in an effort to bring chaos

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.820
and violence to Gotham.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.760
But what he really comes off as is just some
"dorky streamer guy," as fans put it on Reddit.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.690
"I just ordered a slice of pumpkin pie."

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:11.110
In a moment where fiction comes hilariously
close to reality, a team of Gotham City police

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:16.260
officers and Batman discover a dark web social
media profile where they learn Riddler's been

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.600
posting regular updates to his 500-plus followers.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.430
It would appear that the main antagonist of
"The Batman" is interacting online with supporters

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.620
as if he's an Instagram or YouTube influencer.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.270
He addresses them by saying,

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.750
"Hey guys, welcome back to my channel."

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.140
The video has racked up tons of heart emojis,
or "likes" from followers who also contribute

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.030
comments to show their allegiance.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:42.110
The video shows Riddler going so far as to
thank his growing brood for, quote, "liking,

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.480
commenting, and subscribing" to his channel
— while also showing his appreciation for

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.690
some advice that people gave him regarding
bomb detonators.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:53.240
“There’s a bomb around his neck!”

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:58.190
"The Batman" fans took to a Reddit post to
discuss this particular scene, pointing out

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:02.979
how the third-act reveal risks neutralizing
any threatening energy Riddler gives off earlier

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:04.229
in the movie.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:05.229
One Redditor wrote,

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:09.349
"I love how he does the creepy Zodiac thing
for the police and the media, but then that

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:13.349
last video for his followers was one of those
dorky 'Hey guys, thanks for the comments,

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:16.730
like and subscribe' videos like every random
YouTuber."

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.110
Another Reddit user replied,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.650
"That dichotomy was so f—ing funny without
it feeling telegraphed or forced."

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.700
Both of the Redditors' comments received more
than 2,000 upvotes combined.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:31.459
What ultimately makes the scene hilarious
for moviegoers is the juxtaposition of Dano's

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:35.810
wild and chaotic masked Riddler with his quiet
side which conjures up comparisons to the

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.830
incel community, as the Redditors put it.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.379
Still, while a lot of filmgoers were laughing
at the scene, many were also deeply disturbed

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:46.900
by it and other clips that the Riddler released
throughout the movie.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.079
One Reddit user admitted,

00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:52.890
"It felt so unnerving and chilling to watch
how Riddler can be this devious yet calculated

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.299
serial killer one moment to this dorky streamer
guy."

00:02:56.299 --> 00:02:57.959
Another person agreed, saying,

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:04.939
"Legit, Paul had the serial killer/incel/school
shooter persona down so well it was both uncanny

00:03:04.939 --> 00:03:06.469
and supremely entertaining."

00:03:06.469 --> 00:03:08.620
A third user chimed in, adding,

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.370
"It was even the same with his face to face
with Batman.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:16.739
You see him just do an about-face from calculated
sociopath to crazy manic on a dime multiple

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:17.739
times.

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:19.230
It was a little bit unsettling."

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.010
According to Collider, Matt Reeves' Riddler
was ultimately inspired by the director's

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:28.590
fascination with the Zodiac Killer, which
grew after reading the 1995 non-fiction book,

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.189
"Mindhunter: Inside the FBI's Elite Serial
Crime Unit."

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:35.239
The book later inspired a Netflix show of
the same name.

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:39.269
What a lot of moviegoers might not know is
that Reeves also intended for his Riddler

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:43.790
to be a mass killer-terrorist of sorts, as
seen when he attacks a Gotham City funeral

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.109
using a car and unwilling victim as the driver.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:48.209
Reeves told Total Film,

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:49.939
"He's not just a serial killer.

00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:51.730
He definitely has a political agenda.

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.930
There's a terrorist aspect to him."

00:03:53.930 --> 00:04:01.639
"The riddles were both, uh, fun but also emotional
and they are layered themselves."

